   "His growing deafness carried particular consequences for a musician whose professional life depended on listening, teaching and performing. At Heiligenstadt he sought treatment and a measure of distance from Viennese society.",
   "The manuscript did not become a letter delivered to the brothers. It was both a communication with named addressees and a record intended, at least in part, to explain his life after his death.",
   "Its anguished language is firsthand evidence of his distress. Yet not sending it does not prove one simple motive, and the manuscript itself should not be reduced to a permanent portrait of despair.",
   "The preserved text, dates and archival material at Beethoven-Haus confirm the document's existence and form. An invented scene of him sealing an envelope or deciding against a postbox would add unsupported drama.",
   "Beethoven continued to compose for decades and the text became known after his death. Later generations met a private voice that its named readers had not received at the time."
